Context: Grothendieck is building a purely combinatorial 'analysis situs' of figures on a line. The base is a totally ordered finite set L of points (M_0) together with the open intervals S_{a,b} between them (M_1). A 'préfigure' 𝔓 is a subset of M = M_0 ∪ M_1, that is, a finite union of points and open intervals. Its closure 𝔓̄ adds the endpoints of its intervals. The set of vertices is δ𝔓 = 𝔓̄ ∩ L.

The previous page defines the order ord(a,𝔓) as the number of intervals X ∈ 𝔓_1 having a as an endpoint (a ◁ X). This order is 0, 1 or 2. Order 0 gives isolated vertices (which must lie in 𝔓), order 1 gives boundary vertices, order 2 gives interior vertices. A vertex is 'proper' if it lies in 𝔓_0 = 𝔓 ∩ L and 'improper' otherwise.

This page completes the zoology. Proper interior vertices are called 'redundant' or 'sommets de morcellement' (subdivision vertices): they merely cut a connected open arc into two intervals. Improper interior vertices are called 'lacunary': they are punctures. The example is a figure a —( )— b —(•)— c —( d, plus an isolated point e. It realises all five combinatorial types:
- e: isolated;
- a: proper boundary;
- d: improper boundary;
- c: proper interior (redundant);
- b: improper interior (lacunary).

Terminology is then fixed. S_{a,b} = S_{b,a} = S_{{a,b}} is to be called an 'interval' rather than a 'segment'. The 'segment' is S̃_{a,b} = {S_{a,b}, a, b}, i.e. the full closure S̄_{a,b}. He recalls 𝔓_0 = 𝔓 ∩ M_0 = 𝔓 ∩ L (the proper vertices) and 𝔓_1 = 𝔓 ∩ M_1.

A crossed-out passage begins to classify the intervals of 𝔓:
- 'closed in 𝔓' when X̄ ⊂ 𝔓, i.e. ∂X ⊂ 𝔓_0;
- 'semi-open' when ∂X ∩ 𝔓_0 is a single endpoint.

Purpose: this sets up the next page's normal form. There, isolated points are removed and redundant points erased, giving a disjoint union of closed segments (a flag in M_1). 𝔓 is then recovered by marking the extra points (isolated and interior ones). The whole is a combinatorial description of finite unions of cells in dimension 1, i.e. constructible or 'tame' subsets of a line.
